/-!
# Finite free algebras with field closed fibre over a DVR

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 09II, proof of
`proposition-epp-essentially-finite-type`, the p-base preparation paragraph; Algebra,
Tag 00PD, `lemma-characterize-dvr` (blueprint: lem-sfb-finite-free-field-fibre-dvr).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v w

namespace Algebra

/-- A finite free algebra of positive rank over a DVR, with field closed fibre, is a DVR
whose maximal ideal is generated by the image of the base uniformizer.
Source: Stacks Project, Tag 09II, proof of `proposition-epp-essentially-finite-type`,
p-base preparation; the finite-stage argument uses Tag 00PD, `lemma-characterize-dvr`.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-finite-free-field-fibre-dvr)

Proof:
1. Fix the finite nonempty basis `b`. Since `A` is a domain, coordinates show that
   multiplication by any nonzero element of `A` is injective on `B`. A basis vector
   is nonzero, so the algebra map is injective. Thus the image `t` of the uniformizer
   is nonzero, and multiplication by every power of `t` is injective.
2. The basis makes `B` module-finite, hence integral by
   lem-weil-pbc-finite-algebra-is-integral. By
   lem-weil-pbc-integral-maximal-contracts-maximal, every maximal ideal contracts to
   the maximal ideal of `A`, and therefore contains `tB`. The field quotient makes
   `tB` maximal, so it is the unique maximal ideal and `B` is local.
3. The PID `A` is Noetherian by lem-weil-pbc-pid-noetherian-normal. Apply
   lem-weil-pbc-dvr-unit-times-uniformizer-power to the nonzero coordinates of any
   nonzero `x`. Divide by the least uniformizer power among them to write `x = t^n y`.
   At least one coordinate of `y` is a unit, so `y` does not belong to `tB`.
   It is a unit by lem-weil-pbc-local-ring-unit-criterion.
4. For nonzero `x = t^n u` and `z = t^m v`, their product is nonzero: multiplication
   by `t^(n+m)` is injective, and `u*v` is a unit in the nontrivial ring `B`.
   Nontriviality follows from the nonzero field quotient. Thus `B` is a domain.
5. In each nonzero ideal choose an element with least exponent in step 3. Removing
   its unit factor shows that `t^n` is in the ideal, and minimality shows that every
   element is divisible by `t^n`. Thus every ideal is principal, including the zero
   ideal. Its unique maximal ideal `tB` is nonzero by step 1 and proper by the field
   quotient. These are exactly the conditions of def-sc-discrete-valuation-ring. -/
theorem finiteFreeFieldFibre_dvr
    (A : Type u) [CommRing A] [IsDomain A] [IsDiscreteValuationRing A]
    (π : A) (hπ : IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A = Ideal.span {π})
    (B : Type v) [CommRing B] [Algebra A B]
    (ι : Type w) [Finite ι] [Nonempty ι] (b : Module.Basis ι A B)
    (hfibre : IsField (B ⧸ Ideal.span {algebraMap A B π})) :
    Function.Injective (algebraMap A B) ∧
      ∃ (_ : IsDomain B) (_ : IsDiscreteValuationRing B),
        IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal B = Ideal.span {algebraMap A B π} := by
  sorry

end Algebra
